ght but you end up getting a  reorientation of the gradient itself and  the tube shape um that we have here  really facilitates a smooth orientation  of that and you actually end up getting  an expansion of those bands upon  reorientation  and so really higher resolution is  available  um using these vertical rotors  especially when you're separating  particles that differ uh in differ in  terms of density on a pretty small scale  so things like partial and full capsids  for viral vectors for example  so  um looking in more detail about some of  these rotors and these are the most  common ones  I've already talked about each of these  a little bit but in terms of volume uh  the vti 50.1 is the newest rotor that  we've commercialized and it's got a  larger volume than any of the other ones  that I've talked about so far today  there's actually 12 by 39 mil capacity  for a total of nearly 500 mils  and if you look at how long it will take  you for an isopic separation in each of  each of these rotors that's laid out  there in the bottom of that table where  vti 50.1 is very clearly the fastest and  it also provides the best resolution  uh and so you can really form these  gradients faster get a higher resolution  and uh in addition to that these this  new rotor uh uses basically all the same  uh consumables tubes adapters everything  that that  um the rest of the rotors use  uh so now I can jump into some of these  case studies uh where we actually  provide some examples and specifics to  illustrate some of these things that  I've talked about so far  uh so this first one here uh was done  using that vti 50.1 rotor that I've  talked a lot about  um and what we start with there on the  top left is a uniform distribution of  our sample which in this case is  adenovirus serotype five  as well as cesium chloride and this was  done at a defined starting density of  1.35 grams per ml uh that should be  pretty close to the buoyant density of  adenovirus  we wrap this up as quickly as possible  uh in the xpn  um the Optima xpn centrifuge and we did  this exact same separation in a handful  of different rotors so starting at the  top we've got the swinging bucket in the  middle of the fixed angle and then at  the bottom the vti 50.1 or the vertical  rotor which we ran at two speeds  we then applied a maximum break to this  as I mentioned before and we measured  the position of each of these bands and  Quantified them using DLS  uh and then in those  um images that you can see there at the  bottom uh so the  um  fixed angle is the farthest on the left  there you see two distinct bands and  then farthest on the right is The  Swinging bucket rotor where you again  see two distinct bands what I really  want to point out here is that when we  did these separations in the vertical  rotor  we actually ended up seeing three  distinct bands for each of them uh and  so emergence of a third band is possible  uh again that's because of the higher  resolution that is is uh achievable  using these types of rotors so  um really a strong use case here for  vertical rotors when it comes to isopic  gradients  now jumping to the next one here  um this is really meant to not instead  of illustrating the resolution  illustrate the  um the time that it takes to get to  equilibrium with these rotors so same  type of layout as the previous slide but  instead there on the top left we're not  starting with a homogeneous  concentration so we did play a bit of a  trick here  where we're starting with layers so  we've got one layer at the bottom which  is a high density cesium chloride  solution  and then at the top is our sample  and we threw that in the vti 90 rotor uh  and did the exact same process as before  where we tried out a handful of  different situa um uh run speeds and and  times  then apply to maximum break and from  there it recovered the sample or  fractionated the tube and this was done  via manual fractionation  and if you look at that graph there at  the bottom you can see this is the  equilibrium scenario so this is what a  lot of customers have approached us  about you're running this thing for a  long time often overnight for 16 hours  and you can see the the gradient there  um in those gray dots so really you can  you can take a look at the range of that  gradient as well as the profile  but when we start to implement some  multi-speed runs so starting it at a  very high high speed and dropping it  down or  um adjusting those times as much as we  can  um you can see that in this case here  with the blue uh the blue dots there now  30 minutes at 90k and then an hour at  60k we get pretty close to where we were  with the 16 hour time point  now speed of a rotor is directly  correlated to the resolution That You  observe so higher speeds tend to favor  better sensitivity while lower speeds  favor better resolution  and so in this case  um you can see that there's quite a bit  of difference uh it's it's close but  it's not exactly where we want it to be  to get that exact uh exact same gradient  profile so we tried another run here  um there in red where we ran for 50  minutes at 90k and 30 minutes at 60k and  we see there that it's quite similar to  what we saw with the 16 hour run at 60k  so starting at a really high speed and  then dropping it down can allow you to  form these gradients faster and in this  particular case we were able to form  that continuous gradient in just 80  minutes using this vti 90 rotor  so now I'll jump into some of the key  takeaways from uh the presentation today  so starting here with  um  the instrument and some of the tubes  that are offered so our Flagship  instrument here is the Optima xpn which  is uh GMP supporting and we've got a ton  of tubes that are available for a  variety of different rotors these are  just five of the many options that we've  got at the smaller scale two mil tubes  which we've seen a lot of customers  Implement very successfully for uh sort  of process development and scaling up  and then on that larger scale once  you've developed a process up to the 39  mil tubes which is what our vti 50.1  rotor uses we've also got a fixed angle  rotor that uses 100 Mil tubes  and then in terms of tube format so  quick seal is probably the most common  thing that we've got especially for the  highest bio containment on the left  there is polypropylene which is really  um broadly compatible with a variety of  different materials  and then on the right side they're Ultra  clear and so you can see an example of  the visibility that's offered by these  types of tubes  uh we also sell Opti seal tubes  um and these are plug seal so much  simpler than what you get with the quick  seal not quite as high of Bio  containment but really ideal for fast  simple Ultra workflows especially at  that that r d type scale and environment  um we also offer open top tubes but  obviously those those won't work uh in a  vertical rotor  and in terms of the rotors themselves  these are the four that we've got  available today I've talked a little bit  about the vti 90 and and how quickly you  can form a continuous gradient in that  uh and this is uh using those smaller  scale tubes in the middle there with the  65.2 and 65.1 we've got  um a pretty good balance between speed  and uh number of samples or throughput  and then finally uh all the way on the  right there's the vti 50.1 which is the  um largest capacity vertical rotor on  the market  so to wrap things up here  um I'll go through some of these  conclusions so  density gradients are are typically  chosen when you've got a relatively  difficult separation  um in other words there's a very subtle  difference between what you're trying to  separate out and what you're trying to  purify and also because of high Purity  and high yield so some examples of this  are are viral vectors plasmids  extracellular vesicles  and then within that dguc realm rate  zonal runs are which are a separation  based on size and mass or sedimentation  coefficient  are best suited for swinging bucket  rotors  and then isopic or equilibrium zonal  runs  um in other words a separation basis on  the basis of buoyant density  is done using vertical rotors  so to go over some of the benefits of  these vertical rotors  um first of all it's high Purity I  talked about the shallow density  gradients that are formed because  there's a pretty minimal shift in um  centrifugal force between the inner and  outer radius of the tubes so those offer  the highest resolution  in terms of yield these particles really  are getting compressed into these these  very discrete type bands and so very  often it's very easy easy to visualize  these and precisely extract them from  the tube  run times are shorter in these vertical  rotors as well because you can achieve  equilibrium faster  in terms of capacity I already mentioned  the 50.1 is the highest capacity  vertical rotor on the market and then  finally with dguc as a whole  these techniques are serotype  independent right all three of the ones  I've discussed today so each tube in a  single run could be a different sample  or a different stereotype and the  highest uh  different sample type rotor that we've  got is for 16. so really great in an r d  environment as well as in larger scale  workflows as well  and then finally  I've also mentioned how these cesium  chloride density gradients can be  reduced in 80 minutes using the smaller  scale vti 90 rotor it's really fantastic  for sort of building out your process at  that smaller scale and not using quite  as much sample to do it and then scaling  up from there is is quite simple as the  process Remains the Same the only thing  that has to be accounted for is slightly  different speeds and dimensions in the  tubes that you're using  so with that I'd like to  um thank thank everyone for your  attention today and uh just briefly  mention that we've got a lot of  additional content that's available on  vertical rotors as well as density  gradient Ultra centrifugation at large  um so I'm just sharing some of these  here but if you go to our website we can  act you can access a lot more of those  thank you  thank you Sean for your informative  presentation if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now just  click the ask a question box located on  the far left of your screen we'll answer  as many questions as we have time for  let's get started our first question is  what purification scale is possible with  density gradients how much time do the  vertical rotors save  uh thank you that's a that's a great  question and and one that we get quite  often uh really it comes down to what  you're trying to purify and and what  your workflow looks like  um I tend to always default to viral  vectors that's what I hear most people  working on these days  um and so we've got these vertical  rotors implemented in r d workflows and  I've already talked a little bit about  um how the serotype independent nature  of these techniques is an advantage  there  um you know every every hole in the  rotor can be something different but  then to your question all the way to PD  and Manufacturing  um 50 liter bioreactors are commonly  purified in a single run but we've also  got a lot of customers that have really  strong concentration steps prior to  centrifugation so typically this is done  using tff or tangential flow filtration  um or on on the smaller scale if you've  got a need for that centrifugal spin  filters work quite well and so with that  concentration step we've got customers  that are able to do 200 liter reactors  in a single rotor especially with the  the higher volume one  um and then the the second part of that  question is as far as Time Savings goes  again it's somewhat case uh Case by case  um but as I mentioned before many of our  customers are running these dguc  experiments overnight so 16 to 22 hours  for cesium chloride tends to be pretty  common  um but what that tells me is that  there's a lot of room for optimization  and Time Savings in many of these cases  I shared the example for  uh the the case study there with with  CBM uh where we saw really strong  purification and separation between  those species in in uh six to nine hours  and then on the smaller scale  um that vti 90 rotor can form a gradient  and a linear gradient in 80 minutes so  there really is a big potential for Time  Savings in most cases by switching to  these rotors but speed has to be  accounted for as well  great thank you uh my next question do  you have any tips for recovering sample  after dguc  yeah this tends to be one of the pain  points in the process I don't think  anyone really loves the way things are  currently done  um one thing that's often overlooked I  think is uh  the lab setup and the layout that that  can be really crucial for this uh so  what I mean by that is setting the  centrifuges close as possible to the  hood or or where you're actually doing  the work in terms of extracting your  sample from it you want to make sure  you're not moving these tubes around too  much after the gradient has been formed  um two common methods for this are as I  mentioned extraction and fractionation  uh so extraction is just using a syringe  and a needle to actually pluck out the  the band that you see there  um and one of the things that helps a  lot with that is for uh is putting a  light underneath the tube so there's  some flat kind of lights that we use I  think I purchased them on Amazon for our  lab and those can slide right underneath  pretty well and illuminate things and  allow you to see it see the band quite a  bit better  uh and then in terms of  um material so  we offer polypropylene and Ultra clear  polypropylene is is very compatible with  with most solutions and Ultra clear  tends to provide higher visibility but I  think in terms of piercing the tube  itself uh polypropylene is is a little  bit easier to do  and then also I I briefly mentioned this  but fractionation using a chromatography  system is becoming more and more popular  as kind of an automated approach to to  actually doing this  thank you  um do you have any recommendations or  suggestions for separating precursor  phage  um capsids that have different amounts  of protein that can be an ex  um unexpanded semi-expanded and fully  expanded and no DNA using the vertical  rotors  that's an interesting application  um it sounds like there would be some  discrete differences in density between  each of those different species so I  would assume a density gradient probably  linear given that there's a lot of  different subpopulations there would  work quite well for this and obviously  as I said the vertical rotors tend to be  the best for these types of  purifications in terms of  recommendations I'd have to try to  understand your workflow a bit better  but perhaps that's something we can chat  about offline I can I can follow up with  you  great  um in the first case study why is there  only one lower band visible with the  50.2  uh yeah so that's the the 50.2 is the  fixed angle rotor that we used in there  um and the thought there is that it just  simply hasn't reached equilibrium yet  um you know the sample was exactly the  same going into all of those tubes and  rotors uh so really I think it's just  that the material that makes up that  band has not been compressed into a  tight band yet and that's  probably due to the longer path length  um that those fixed angled rotors have  compared to the vertical  great thank you our next question what  kind of throughput is possible using  dguc  this is another common question we get  to  um a bit of a tricky one but I'm  assuming you're meaning a number of  samples here so of the vertical rotor  options that we have uh the vti 65.2 is  the most samples so you can run up to 16  samples at once  um  and it won't be able to purify quite as  fast as the vti 90 and in terms of  thinking in terms of throughput here but  I would assume within a few hours you'd  be good to go if you have an optimized  optimized method using that rotor  great  um our next question is gradient  reorientation a problem in vertical  rotors wouldn't swinging bucket be  better  um not really the the tubes that we have  are are designed to allow for that  smooth reorientation and going back  again to that that first case study it  might have been a bit hard to see in the  images  but that was Illustrated kind of in  there where we've got  um visible bands looking exactly the  same in both of the rotors the swing  bucket or vertical so reorientation  doesn't seem to really play a role there  in terms of what the output actually is  uh and then also the max break thing too  I do want to point that out one more  time because it's often surprising when  I talk to people about this but that  study was done using applying maximum  break so no coasting at all  um uh for everything so really a I don't  think there's a need to have that extra  long step there at the end in most cases  our next question my ultra doesn't have  the option of doing a multi-speed  protocol why  so for the Beckman  um floor models we've got two that we  that we sell and those are the XE and  the xpn  um the XE tends to be a little bit  cheaper but the xpn has that multi-speed  option along with some other improved  software features including kind of a  smart software as well as more the GMP  supporting so it's probably that you  have the the XE rather than the xpn  and it looks like we have one more  question here can you elaborate on  breaking and coasting to stop the run  yeah um  coasting has always been the traditional  approach I think for this  um and depending on the speed that  you're running at and the type of rotor  that you use uh it can take over an hour  sometimes uh and on our instruments  um I think there's either nine or ten  different options to choose from in  terms 